      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Catherine Baker Chatman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit decision in the court below was wrong, for three reasons. It adopted a rule that frustrates Congress's intent to protect Federal Courts from hearing stale claims and to respect the finality of State Court convictions. It does so by improperly and arbitrarily adopting a conclusive presumption that misunderstands or ignores State law and practice. And it is inconsistent with this Court's decision in Carey versus Saffold."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But that's something -- where would we -- we would just pick that number, that"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I don't know how this came about."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I don't know what it --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stris",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STRIS: It presumes that if it's within 90 days, it's timely."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Stris",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STRIS: The burden shifts the prisoner to produce some evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0003-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And, in fact, if the Federal Courts are required to\nreview these cases, they'll be required to do it in 60\ndays, in 70 days, in 80 days, and, if they make a mistake,\nand they find a case to be untimely that the\nCalifornia Court didn't, will deprive first Federal\nhabeas, one Congress didn't intend."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Chatman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One thing I'd like to address is the capital-\ncase presumption, which is now -- excuse me -- 180\ndays. It's been changed from 90 days. That\npresumption is for -- from the filing of the reply\nbrief to filing an initial petition in California\nCourt. That is, your first habeas petition. That --\nyou only get presumed timely for 180 days."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Why? What would the theory be? I don't understand that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Chatman",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The other thing I'd like to address is the\nargument that a State Court is saying something with\nthese summary orders. I strongly disagree with that.\nThey are absolutely saying nothing. This Court said in\nYlst that the essence of an unexplained order is that\nit says nothing. It does not say, \"This is a timely\npetition.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-721",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6b04c4f137986662247c0f61f1282b87dd473a0dfaef47f676ba175a7ee5e3f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6b04c4f13798-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted.\n[Whereupon, at 12:03 p.m., the case in the\nabove-entitled matter was submitted.]"
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
